"fee-calculator":{g:"Tools",eyebrow:"Tool",h1:"Fee model <em>calculator</em>",lede:"See how a fixed fee, a hybrid and a pure profit-share compare over your horizon — and the gross return at which they cost the same.",s:[{t:"feecalc"}],rel:["fee-model","direct-plan"]},
"growth-calculator":{g:"Tools",eyebrow:"Tool",h1:"Growth <em>calculator</em>",lede:"Project an investment at a fund's since-inception return against its benchmark and a fixed deposit. Illustrative — the past is not a promise.",s:[{t:"growthcalc"}],rel:["reading-returns","pms"]},
"wrapper-quiz":{g:"Tools",eyebrow:"Tool",h1:"Which wrapper <em>fits</em> me?",lede:"Four questions about your money and temperament. We'll point you to a starting wrapper and matching funds.",s:[{t:"quiz"}],rel:["compare-wrappers","first-time"]},
tax:{g:"Tools",eyebrow:"Tool · updated September 2026",h1:"What you keep <em>after tax</em>",lede:"The same 20% gross return leaves very different amounts in your hands depending on the wrapper. Category III AIFs pay tax inside the fund; PMS gains are taxed in yours. Here is the arithmetic.",
 facts:[["Listed equity LTCG","12.5%"],["Listed equity STCG","20%"],["Cat III fund-level","Maximum marginal rate"],["Surcharge cap on CG","15%"]],
 s:[
  {t:"prose",h:"The one distinction that matters",b:"<p>Every wrapper falls on one side of a single line: <b>pass-through</b> or <b>fund-level</b>.</p><p>In a pass-through structure — AIF Category I and II under Section 115UB, and PMS, where you own the shares outright — income keeps its character on the way to you. A long-term capital gain stays a long-term capital gain, taxed at your rate, in your return.</p><p>Category III AIFs are the exception. The fund is normally assessed as an indeterminate trust and pays tax itself at the maximum marginal rate on everything it earns. The NAV you see is already net of that. It is why a Category III return of 18% and a PMS return of 18% are not the same number, and should never be compared as though they were.</p>"},
  {t:"taxmatrix"},
  {t:"taxcalc"},
  {t:"prose",h:"Three things investors get wrong",b:"<h3>Comparing a Cat III NAV with a PMS return</h3><p>Cat III performance is post-tax at the fund. PMS performance is pre-tax at your end. Before comparing, reduce the PMS figure by your own expected tax, or gross up the Cat III one.</p><h3>Ignoring churn in a PMS</h3><p>You are taxed on every sale the manager makes, not on your holding period. A high-turnover strategy can hand you a short-term capital gains bill at 20% each year even if you never withdraw a rupee. Ask for portfolio turnover before you sign.</p><h3>Assuming the surcharge is uncapped</h3><p>Surcharge on capital gains from listed equity is capped at 15% even where your slab surcharge is 25% or 37%. That cap is why the effective long-term rate lands near 14.95% for most large portfolios rather than something far higher.</p>"},
  {t:"faq",items:[["Are GIFT City funds tax-free?","Not universally. Sections 10(4D), 10(4E) and 10(23FF) exempt specified income of non-resident investors in IFSC funds, and Category II IFSC funds face a concessional rate. Resident investors are generally taxed under normal Indian rules, and your country of residence may tax the same income. This is the area where professional advice pays for itself."],["How is an NRI taxed on a PMS?","Gains arise in India and attract tax here, with TDS under Sections 195 and 196D. A tax treaty between India and your country of residence usually gives credit for tax paid, so you are not taxed twice, but the paperwork matters."],["Does a SIF get mutual fund treatment?","SIFs sit inside the mutual fund framework, so equity-oriented schemes are taxed like equity mutual funds. CBDT has been finalising the detail; confirm the current position before you invest."],["Is the July 2025 Delhi High Court ruling settled?","No. It opened the possibility of concessional rates for determinate Category III trusts. Until the position is settled, assume maximum marginal rate at fund level."]]}
 ],rel:["reading-returns","compare-wrappers","fee-calculator"]},
